exercises.solution:
```python
def word_counts(text):
    counts = {}
    for word in text.lower().split():
        word = word.strip(".,!?;:")
        if word:                      # skip anything that was only punctuation
            counts[word] = counts.get(word, 0) + 1
    return counts
```

`lower()` makes `Go`, `go` and `GO` the same word; `split()` with no argument splits at any run of whitespace, including new lines; `strip` with an argument removes any of those characters from the two ends. The counting line uses `get(word, 0)` so that a word seen for the first time starts from 0 instead of raising a `KeyError`. The `if word:` guard skips a "word" that was nothing but punctuation, such as a lone `!`.

